{"title":"1967 AHSME Problems/Problem 21","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_AHSME_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In right triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> the hypotenuse <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f324b9900f1b50b8b18638082abf8ce9b6d819b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}=5$\" width=\"61\" height=\"15\" /> and leg <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/1/2f1bf2bac5baf2c36a80b3e39a48498ba4df2084.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AC}=3$\" width=\"60\" height=\"15\" />.  The bisector of angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> meets the opposite side in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36c7f3f828cf12ab9cafeffad282468a95219600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" />.  A second right triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/c/91c587b0c1e5de0bde79b9d8c36913c16dee04d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQR$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"16\" /> is then constructed with hypotenuse <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/a/f2ae1b12c41359402d5789653544ed1178df951b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{PQ}=A_1B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"88\" height=\"19\" /> and leg <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/8/19849c406748f252ecadc7f8e4590fb5bcb8adfd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{PR}=A_1C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" />.  If the bisector of angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> meets the opposite side in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b0256a78d58b6d5f7a53400b19e7747d2983fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"17\" />, the length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/2/6b240c232d851f8ce3dfb1c24c8c3dc7e78a58c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PP_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"31\" height=\"15\" /> is:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/2/942da03385efa4875a44192bd5b8913ce2f8450f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\qquad \\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{4}\\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{15\\sqrt{2}}{16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"498\" height=\"41\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_AHSME_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c3218ca790f4eac4048f34f68433655b19e1f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\fbox{B}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"23\" height=\"23\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_AHSME_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-e